# .w7p5a-real-run-leg-blocklist -- W7P5-a's real-run-LEG BLOCKER register, distinct
# from .w7p5a-real-run-blocklist.
#
# W7P5-a ("agent/plans/PLAN-tooling-transformation.md") sets the bar as "golden dry-run
# parity plus one real run each". The 32 paths in .w7p5a-real-run-blocklist have
# neither leg done directly (their dry-run parity is inherited from W7P6's port);
# the paths here are different: EACH ALREADY CARRIES ITS OWN w7p5a K=5 shadow-gate
# ledger (recorded in .ci/shadow/w7p5a-status.json as `status: "ledger"`), so their
# dry-run-parity leg is genuinely done, under this box's own name. What remains
# blocked is narrower: their real-run branch invokes a real external system
# (a production CDN, a live Cloudflare Worker preview URL, or the real GitHub repo
# via `gh`) that this session has no standing authorization to touch for a porting
# exercise, even for a read-only GET.
#
# WHY THIS CANNOT SHARE A FILE WITH .w7p5a-real-run-blocklist.
# check_w7p5a_real_run_blockers.py's existing STALE check treats ANY entry in that
# file whose path has graduated to "ledger" status as a leftover to be deleted --
# correct for THAT file, since it BLOCKs a whole path's port and a ledgered path's
# port is no longer blocked. Putting a real-run-LEG block in the same file would
# make every entry here look like exactly that leftover. This file's entries are
# never stale merely for being "ledger" status; they are checked by the SEPARATE
# fifth clause in w7p5a_real_run_blockers.py that cross-references this file
# against each `ledger` row's `note` field instead.
#
# Liveness is enforced the same way as .w7p5a-real-run-blocklist: an entry is live
# while (a) the path still exists, (b) .ci/shadow/w7p5a-status.json still records it
# as "ledger" without the real-run confirmation phrase in its `note`, and (c) its
# reason still validates through the canonical rediacc_ci.core.allowlist checker.
# Format: name-per-line, each preceded by one or more BLOCKER-reason comment lines.
